  The first thing I need to resolve is bigger than notifications. "Notify users when tasks they care about change"
  presupposes three things this repo doesn't have: tasks, users, and a definition of "change." Notifications are a
  [3mreaction[0m to a data model, so the data model determines nearly everything about the notification design — whether
  there's a server at all, whether delivery can be push or must be poll, whether "users" plural even means multiple
  people.

  [1mThe fork:[0m what am I building notifications [3mon top of[0m?

  - [1mReal app exists elsewhere[0m — this repo is a stub or a fresh extraction, and the actual tasks/users/API live in
    another codebase. Buys the most accurate design; costs you pointing me at it. Cheapest path by far, because I'd
    design to a real schema instead of inventing one.
  - [1mBuild the task app too[0m — notifications are the headline, but the task CRUD, persistence, and user model come with
    it. Buys a working end-to-end product; costs a much larger scope that I'd want to decompose into sequenced
    sub-projects (tasks first, notifications second), each with its own spec.
  - [1mGreenfield, notifications-first[0m — we design the task/user model [3mspecifically[0m to serve notifications, building only
    the minimum task surface needed. Buys focus; costs a task model shaped by one consumer, which tends to need rework
    when real task features land.

  [1mMy recommendation: the first, if it's true.[0m Designing a notification system against an imagined task schema is how you
  get a subsystem that doesn't fit the app it's for. If there's no other codebase, I'd take the second and decompose —
  but I want to know which world we're in before spending questions on delivery channels and digest windows.
